# Line endings
#
# Before this file existed the repository stored a per-file mix: most blobs were
# LF, four were CRLF, and Program.cs and TaskFlow.Api.csproj were mixed inside a
# single file. That mix is invisible on Windows, where core.autocrlf hides it,
# but it turns a small edit into a whole-file diff whenever a tool writes the
# other ending — a 26-line change to Program.cs once showed up as 178 changed
# lines, which makes a commit unreviewable.
#
# text=auto: git normalises every text file to LF in the repository and checks it
# out with the platform's native endings. Windows working copies stay CRLF, the
# Linux CI runner gets LF, and the stored blob is the same either way.
* text=auto

# Spelled out per type so a new tool cannot guess wrong about a file git has not
# seen before.
*.cs       text diff=csharp
*.csproj   text
*.slnx     text
*.json     text
*.md       text
*.yml      text
*.yaml     text
*.http     text
*.sql      text
.gitignore text

# Shell scripts must be LF on every platform or the interpreter chokes on the
# carriage return. None exist yet; this is here so the first one is correct.
*.sh       text eol=lf

# Windows scripts are the mirror image.
*.cmd      text eol=crlf
*.bat      text eol=crlf

# Binary — never normalise, never diff as text. None are committed today, but
# these are the types a portfolio repo tends to acquire (an architecture diagram
# is on the roadmap).
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.ico  binary
*.pdf  binary
*.zip  binary
*.dll  binary
*.exe  binary
*.snk  binary
